1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:03,440
That is a Tesla Roadster leaving a ramp,

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:05,240
rolling over in midair…

3
00:00:05,260 --> 00:00:06,600
and landing.

4
00:00:06,620 --> 00:00:09,040
At the same reveal, it hung upside down,

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,120
and Tesla timed a zero-to-sixty run at

6
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:12,840
about one second.

7
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:14,760
Three astonishing clips.

8
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:16,720
Three different engineering tricks.

9
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:18,750
So what exactly flew?

10
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,120
This was Tesla's Roadster reveal on

11
00:00:21,140 --> 00:00:23,920
October 15th, at SpaceX's test site in

12
00:00:23,940 --> 00:00:25,440
McGregor, Texas.

13
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:27,120
Everything you're seeing is a controlled

14
00:00:27,140 --> 00:00:28,880
stunt on a closed course,

15
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:30,960
not a car on a public street.

16
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:32,720
The car drove up a curved ramp and

17
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:35,680
stopped, upside down, for a few seconds.

18
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,760
On another run, it launched off,

19
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:39,973
rolled itself upright, and set down on the

20
00:00:39,993 --> 00:00:40,880
pavement.

21
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,180
It even hopped over a row of Teslas,

22
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:44,530
a Semi included.

23
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:47,000
This Roadster was first promised back in

24
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:48,360
2017.

25
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:51,670
Tesla now says production starts in 2028.

26
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,180
Start with the upside-down trick,

27
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:55,860
because it's the cleverest.

28
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:57,900
Under that ramp, gravity pulls the car

29
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,580
away with its full weight.

30
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:00,940
That's one g.

31
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:03,180
To stay put, something has to push back

32
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,220
harder.

33
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:06,180
Tesla says fans pull air out from under

34
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:07,180
the car.

35
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,020
That leaves a thin layer of low-pressure

36
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:10,940
air between the floor and the ramp,

37
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,740
while the air on the other side stays at

38
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:13,820
normal pressure…

39
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:16,020
and normal air pressure is surprisingly

40
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:16,780
strong.

41
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:18,460
The difference shoves the car into the

42
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:19,500
surface.

43
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:21,660
Tesla claims the system can pull up to two

44
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,380
g: double what it needs to hang there.

45
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,260
Racing teams tried fan cars back in the

46
00:01:26,280 --> 00:01:29,020
1970s, and they gripped so well that the

47
00:01:29,040 --> 00:01:30,620
rules shut them down.

48
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:33,100
And notice: this suction is a separate

49
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:34,490
system from the jets.

50
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:36,350
Now the jets.

51
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:38,640
These are cold-gas thrusters:

52
00:01:38,660 --> 00:01:40,240
a tank of compressed gas,

53
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,030
some valves, and nozzles.

54
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:43,120
No fuel.

55
00:01:43,140 --> 00:01:44,030
No flame.

56
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:46,240
Push gas out one way, and the car gets

57
00:01:46,260 --> 00:01:48,160
pushed the other way, like letting go of a

58
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:49,190
balloon.

59
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:51,400
Fire a nozzle up on one side and down on

60
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:53,520
the other, and the car rolls.

61
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:55,360
Fire the opposite pair, and the roll

62
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:56,390
stops.

63
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:58,400
Tesla says it worked with SpaceX to make

64
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:00,320
thrusters like these work in air,

65
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:01,710
not just in space.

66
00:02:02,020 --> 00:02:04,320
Watch the launch again: the ramp provides

67
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:05,800
the speed and the height,

68
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,960
and the jets help manage the rotation and

69
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:09,030
the landing.

70
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:10,880
What none of this shows is sustained

71
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,080
flight, a useful flying range,

72
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:14,880
or anything certified to carry people

73
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:15,950
through the air.

74
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:18,420
Then there's the acceleration.

75
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,260
In the run at the event, the on-screen

76
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:22,530
timer stopped at one second flat.

77
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:24,660
Tesla says it has measured as little as

78
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,780
0.81 seconds but that's Tesla's own

79
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,020
number, and nobody independent has timed

80
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:30,650
it.

81
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:33,380
For perspective, even one second averages

82
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,780
almost three times the pull of gravity:

83
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,860
far more than ordinary road tires can grip

84
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:38,690
on their own.

85
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,620
So Tesla leans on thrusters here too.

86
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,460
It says they push harder than the tires

87
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:44,180
could.

88
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,050
And that's where the versions split.

89
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:48,500
Performance is the road-ready two-seater,

90
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:49,810
with the suction system.

91
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:52,610
Thrust adds rear cold-gas thrusters.

92
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,340
Rocket is invite-only, seats one,

93
00:02:55,360 --> 00:02:57,180
and has the full flight system…

94
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,940
and Tesla's own page says Rocket is not

95
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:01,810
approved for public road use.

96
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:04,690
So, did the Roadster fly?

97
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,340
For a few seconds…

98
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:07,020
yes.

99
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:09,260
A car left the ground and used jets of air

100
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:11,460
to turn itself over and land.

101
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:14,300
That part is real, and it's genuinely new.

102
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,620
The bigger idea, a flying car you can

103
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:19,220
actually drive, is still unproven.

104
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:21,100
And the version that flies isn't approved

105
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:22,260
for the road.

106
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:25,180
Tesla says production starts in 2028.

107
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:26,700
Which matters more to you:

108
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:27,740
the stunt…

109
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:30,480
or the version people can actually drive?

